    "language": [],
    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "While I am interested in representing Hmong people and culture to a larger American nation unfamiliar with who and what the Hmong are, I am also largely invested in empowering the ways in which the Hmong document our stories and remember ourselves through oral or craft storytelling as an act of resistance and resilience. I am a bearer of my ancestors’ art and history, deriving my memories, imagination, and creation from the embroidered textile, or paj ntaub, Hmong people have traditionally labored and hand-crafted—works of art which tell of our everlasting existence despite recurring displacement and diasporas. I make timeless and infinite my Hmong people’s resilience—our ways of being and traversing worlds—through artworks that pull from Hmong history, experiences, and craft.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Yang, G. (2024). What are Hmong People? Hmong People are Resilient: Remembering the Hmong and Their Resiliency Across Time and Place. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/bp050gn6749. https://doi.org/10.25740/bp050gn6749.",
